WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.708
SPEAKER: Welcome to
this episode of Click

00:00:01.708 --> 00:00:03.750
to Deploy, a series
where we show you

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:06.480
how easy it is to deploy
solutions from the Google Cloud

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:07.410
marketplace.

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:09.060
In this episode,
we'll be reviewing

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:13.200
how to set up NetApp Cloud
Volumes ONTAP via the Cloud

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:14.340
Marketplace.

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:17.190
NetApp Cloud Volumes
ONTAP in Google Cloud

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:20.700
is a data management layer that
runs natively on Google Cloud

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.850
infrastructure, enabling
enhanced control,

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:26.520
data protection,
mobility, and agility

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:29.550
for business application data.

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:31.530
Let's start by searching
for a Cloud Volumes

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:33.880
ONTAP in the Google
Cloud marketplace.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:36.960
For this demo, we'll choose
the solution Cloud Manager

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:39.480
for Cloud Volumes ONTAP.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:41.490
The landing page will
display information

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:46.020
about the solution, pricing,
documentation, and support.

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:48.840
To use Cloud Manager
and Cloud Volumes ONTAP,

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:50.760
Google Cloud users
need to subscribe

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:54.540
to Cloud Volumes ONTAP via
the Google Cloud marketplace.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:58.320
Let's click the Subscribe
button to launch the solution.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.420
Start first by choosing
a billing account

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:02.070
and agreeing to the
terms below, which

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:04.379
are terms about subscription
fee billed monthly

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:06.600
and canceling the service.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:08.280
After choosing the
Subscribe button,

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:12.210
you will see a notification
to register with NetApp, Inc.

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:13.890
Before we visit
NetApp, let's make

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:15.750
sure to create a
Google Cloud account

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:20.610
with the necessary Identity and
Access Management, IAM, policy.

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:22.380
This will make it
possible to use

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:25.380
Cloud Manager with your
Google Cloud environment.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:27.480
From this NetApp
website, download

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:30.000
the connector deployment
policy for GCP

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.540
and the Cloud Manager
policy for GCP.

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:36.030
Let's create a custom role
for the connector deployment

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:37.860
and assign it to a user.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:39.750
In the Cloud Shell,
upload the setup

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:42.180
as service version
GCP YAML permission

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:45.150
file you previously downloaded.

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:47.070
For the Cloud Shell,
use this command

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:50.610
to create a custom role for
the file at the organization

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:52.080
or project level.

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:55.260
We'll make this role
at the project level.

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:57.360
The role created using
the permission file

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:00.150
will be titled
NetApp Cloud central.

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:03.000
To grant the new role
created to the relevant user,

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:07.440
you can run the gcloud
addiam-policy-binding command.

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:09.360
Next, let's set up
a service account

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:12.240
that will be associated
with the connector VM.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.510
Start by uploading policy
for Cloud Manager version

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:18.810
gcp.yaml permission
file to the Cloud Shell.

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:21.750
Then go to IAM &amp; Admin,

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:24.150
Navigate to Service
Account, then

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:26.190
Create a New Service Account.

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:30.180
For Service Account Name,
choose NetApp Connector.

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:33.210
For Role, choose
NetApp Cloud Central.

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:36.120
Now, we have a Google Cloud user
with the permissions required

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:38.640
to create a connector
from Cloud Manager,

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.820
as well as a service account
for the connector VM.

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:45.480
Let's enable Cloud API
needed for Cloud Volumes.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:48.240
Go to API &amp; Services tab.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:51.270
We'll need to make sure the
following APIs are enabled--

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:56.370
Cloud Deployment Manager
V2 API, Cloud Logging API,

00:02:56.370 --> 00:03:01.350
Cloud Resource Manager API,
Compute Engine API, Identity

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:04.440
and Access Management, IAM API.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:06.120
Now, let's deploy NetApp.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:09.540
First, go to the Marketplace
Cloud Volumes landing page

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:12.600
and choose Manage on Provider.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:16.060
Create an account or log in
to your existing account.

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:18.540
Next, we'll navigate
to Fabric View

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:21.180
and choose Cloud Volumes ONTAP.

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:23.010
Go to Cloud Manager.

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:25.230
Let's add our first
working environment.

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:28.290
First, we'll choose
Google Cloud platform.

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:31.170
Next, we'll choose
Cloud Volumes ONTAP.

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:33.000
Now, let's create
a connector which

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.120
allows Cloud Manager to
manage resources and processes

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:38.310
within your public
cloud environment.

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:40.440
We'll choose Google
Cloud Platform.

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:42.090
We will need to
provide permissions

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:45.600
to connect our Google
account with Cloud Manager.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:47.910
We'll set our Connector
Instance Name.

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:51.030
Let's use test-connector.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:52.260
We'll set the project.

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:55.500
It will be connected
to test-ctd.

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:58.710
We'll set Surface
Account NetApp Connector.

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:01.140
Next, we'll set the
Location details.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:03.390
I'll keep the defaults.

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:06.660
Then we'll set the Connectivity
to Public IP Enable.

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:10.800
And finally, for the firewall
policy, choose My IP.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:13.680
Now that we filled out all of
this additional information,

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:15.960
make sure to review the details.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:18.720
As you can see, the connector
was successfully created.

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:22.079
Congratulations on successfully
deploying a NetApp Cloud

00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:23.970
Volumes ONTAP instance.

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:25.800
You now have a
platform to simplify

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:28.470
the management of your
workloads on Google Cloud.

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:30.930
If you enjoyed this video,
please subscribe and hit

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:32.113
the Like button below.

00:04:32.113 --> 00:04:34.530
We encourage you to check out
the Google Cloud marketplace

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:36.520
and deploy the
solution on your own.

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:38.540
Thanks for watching.

